#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345005 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3baf4567-d424-573a-9e30-4ff96fa2d669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342729} {:answer/id #uuid "4f893e89-6a93-53ab-b746-44f7cf20c423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342900} {:answer/id #uuid "6f5dd490-0f0f-53bd-b098-70c3ce3d0f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343196} {:answer/id #uuid "9c9e3fe9-3652-5268-9ac3-a883593c3468" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343618} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f15882-a4eb-5654-8f46-170a82fde8d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343658}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343658} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7ab88d0-e46a-5e99-86e7-68aecdee4f07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "56c07c72-df51-5ec4-88ac-9185b7690371" :question/math-academy-id "q-74966" :question/problem "$\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=$" :question/worked-solution "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\frac{2\\cdot 0\\cdot {0}^{3}}{{0}^{6}+8\\cdot {0}^{2}}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nLet's attempt to evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y=x}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{4}}{{x}^{2}({x}^{4}+8)} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{4}+8} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y={x}^{3},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)\\,\\hspace{0.222em}\\hspace{0.222em}y={x}^{3}}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}} & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{{x}^{6}+8{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2{x}^{6}}{9{x}^{6}} \\\\  & =\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}\\hspace{0.167em}\\frac{2y{x}^{3}}{{x}^{6}+8{y}^{2}}=\\text{DNE}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345141 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e7bd1b3-9829-5473-8614-8c430afd35b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$4$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343033} {:answer/id #uuid "6886ac93-669b-567a-bcd2-cdc962ddc459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$6$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343131} {:answer/id #uuid "84a8edc1-a88f-5434-afb0-143e81012a89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343372} {:answer/id #uuid "abe395a2-a154-5294-8225-b25996255c90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $6$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343751} {:answer/id #uuid "d30a39f9-5218-5a0d-9fba-e6226fea77be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ and $3$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344090}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343751} :answer-field/id #uuid "59951fee-072e-57e6-a856-742c18a4ab71" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344644}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e4e0a287-1212-5e98-875c-b8a10ba64112" :question/math-academy-id "q-7595" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be  $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=4,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345157 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b4bbdd7-3b6a-517b-9ed6-1c446dcec71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$5$ and $4$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342857} {:answer/id #uuid "74e98528-55f2-5075-9918-6944dd0fae8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$25$ and $16$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343250} {:answer/id #uuid "93976b13-3f0e-58d6-b564-21e806715d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343531} {:answer/id #uuid "d72c0683-cce5-5c5b-b1ee-f4a799dd53cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$10$ and $8$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344126} {:answer/id #uuid "dc66f534-da0e-5146-9fee-e309f47a07d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$50$ and $32$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344173}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344126} :answer-field/id #uuid "59484f16-7ef8-58ef-819d-3d6908e8ec4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344643}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "548834cd-5f39-5629-b131-aad9f4ac51b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-7601" :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345158 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09932463-c5ae-59d4-9a1f-8f7b93b1e39e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342317} {:answer/id #uuid "0fde284f-a456-53ec-9d8f-0544d1aada84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342375} {:answer/id #uuid "168b385f-5a94-575d-a63a-f2ac457d8eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342440} {:answer/id #uuid "9062242e-5334-5a2e-9f74-55287a13161a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343501} {:answer/id #uuid "e7356bbf-885d-5a76-ba7a-55f84c4f9035" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344267}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343501} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e169c08-9580-5cff-91b2-b2f2a723f60d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344626}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "802974bf-a2fe-59d8-9a6a-a2db7dc5159c" :question/math-academy-id "q-7602" :question/problem "![](images/bc/bcac8eb60e0603ee38bd200b15072685a2bcebb834d490fb4e5a0c318f401096.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=1$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20a06fb7-fb76-56d8-b6e1-f809612a62d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342506} {:answer/id #uuid "25b26601-0bd0-5ba9-8edc-93a16701e235" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342556} {:answer/id #uuid "325931bd-edd2-5aee-b4bb-db09428a96e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{5}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342663} {:answer/id #uuid "d456c171-7bf0-551c-826b-9b80db67e5fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{18}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344101} {:answer/id #uuid "f9b91c50-fe18-5dce-ac87-c7a197511c9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344434}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342506} :answer-field/id #uuid "ffbc37fc-8913-56b6-aa24-4d3f0156cd73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "efae30e0-3c67-576a-b125-57ed6ed688b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-7608" :question/problem "![](images/20/202dec2a2e75aa6720181eb925f98999031453f8f1f8822dd86d7a2baab21396.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=3$ and the vertical radius has length $b=5.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b4c92cd-b35d-5c18-a223-49150a03011a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25\\sqrt{34}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342724} {:answer/id #uuid "9854cc39-fd63-5ffa-8334-c3108e197497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} {:answer/id #uuid "a719e5db-f272-5b05-bc3d-faa57d00c911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343708} {:answer/id #uuid "ac56b14e-40b3-5364-ac94-8f30ec9d2137" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{34}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343754} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f2338d-321a-5c08-ba22-d59d140ae37a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344373}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343574} :answer-field/id #uuid "fe61a69d-0a4f-5fcc-b1ef-4f5a0dca0429" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344938}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1bfd19a9-a685-5ea5-8c9a-dcf991921379" :question/math-academy-id "q-76564" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral of the function $f(x,y)=xy$ along the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,2)$ to $(4,7).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=4\\,i+7j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[4\\,i+7j-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(3i+5j) \\\\  & =(1+3t)\\,i+(2+5t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+3t$ and $y=2+5t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2+5t)j \\\\  & =3i+5j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=xy$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+3t)(2+5t) \\\\  & =15{t}^{2}+11t+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\cdot \\sqrt{34}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(15{t}^{2}+11t+2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}{[5{t}^{3}+\\frac{11}{2}{t}^{2}+2t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{34}(5+\\frac{11}{2}+2)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{25\\sqrt{34}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345231 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d469bce-94af-5634-ab82-5aa6af354e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13\\sqrt{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342616} {:answer/id #uuid "8209f70f-983a-5ae7-86d1-4e93d816e7c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} {:answer/id #uuid "85c4d835-de5f-5323-ac7e-857e358d872b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{13}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343386} {:answer/id #uuid "a5d73c23-a898-521d-b2b3-a61c11d3d91a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343697} {:answer/id #uuid "baa7a4d0-7ea9-5d62-9f82-07ada8a4ece3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343877}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186343353} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b45262a-d4fd-5260-bbcc-f503f4fcadd6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "27ad0c20-f0d9-5a56-ac84-89e9373c74a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-76569" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from$(1,2,0)$ to $(2,1,3).$\n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+2\\,j$ and $b=2\\,i+j+3\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t[(2\\,i+j+3\\,k)-(i+2\\,j)] \\\\  & =(i+2\\,j)+t(i-j+3\\,k) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(2-t)\\,j+3t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t,$ $y=2-t,$ and $z=3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-t)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)k \\\\  & =i-j+3\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(1+t)(2-t)^{2} \\\\  & ={t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\cdot \\sqrt{11}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}{[\\frac{{t}^{4}}{4}-{t}^{3}+4t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{11}(\\frac{1}{4}-1+4)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\sqrt{11}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345232 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9bcbed-0436-52a7-8a8d-e2de93c0b880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} {:answer/id #uuid "368a2103-e780-5582-ad6f-3a21aa3a8980" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342688} {:answer/id #uuid "6dcbd128-6385-59fc-8240-9d369f5dd5c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{5\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343182} {:answer/id #uuid "b163fa1b-99f8-590b-a1f2-94f8b252cb72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343793} {:answer/id #uuid "bc47b99b-71b1-565f-a23d-6d3b20733bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343893}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342362} :answer-field/id #uuid "fed33cfa-1f9a-54f9-9d11-cd56d8af78bc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344940}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "07d5db28-c47d-5f6c-be75-b0e883a1921b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76577" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(-1,2,1)$ to $(1,2,5).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=-i+2\\,j+k$ and $b=i+2\\,j+5\\,k.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t[(i+2\\,j+5\\,k)-(-i+2\\,j+k)] \\\\  & =(-i+2\\,j+k)+t(2\\,i+4\\,k) \\\\  & =(-1+2t)\\,i+2\\,j+(1+4t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=-1+2t,$ $y=2,$ and $z=1+4t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1+2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+4t)k \\\\  & =2\\,i+0+4\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}((-1+2t)+(1+4t))) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}(x+z))\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}$, $\\text{d}u=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi t}{2}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }(-1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{3\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a0917ea-40c5-507b-8924-1137e4b9229d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342322} {:answer/id #uuid "28556a83-5674-5632-a7b0-74ac4c5a6211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342576} {:answer/id #uuid "356c398e-4bd2-59df-a741-dfdcb21b6a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi \\sqrt{10}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342678} {:answer/id #uuid "5abb0cb1-003f-5e6f-b8df-beba529f73a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342996} {:answer/id #uuid "a44d1448-a188-5bd6-bad3-7888bafa60d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343687}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342322} :answer-field/id #uuid "96bfae9f-8994-5c06-b70f-c7f375df05f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2db69cc9-2b8f-5c01-a877-0e37bcf27dc2" :question/math-academy-id "q-76605" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,1)$ to $(2,4).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=i+j$ and $b=2\\,i+4\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =(i+j)+t[2\\,i+4\\,j-(i+j)] \\\\  & =(i+j)+t(i+3\\,j) \\\\  & =(1+t)\\,i+(1+3t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=1+t$ and $y=1+3t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+3t)j \\\\  & =1\\,i+3\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)={sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8}\\right)$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi ((1+3t)-(1+t))}{8}\\right) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (y-x)}{8}\\right)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\cdot \\sqrt{10}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=\\frac{\\pi t}{4},$ $\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{10}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{sec}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{4}\\right)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\operatorname{tan}⁡u\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{tan}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{10}}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345241 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a9d0ea6-699e-529f-8386-4d8ae6eb9a87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2e\\sqrt{2}({e}^{5}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342592} {:answer/id #uuid "530ca827-8329-5ca0-99fc-22a829724023" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342933} {:answer/id #uuid "d9e2be51-e86b-58ab-9a10-7841de2f4293" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344151} {:answer/id #uuid "dfe6f6f8-75fd-55fd-9a9a-45090e8a563d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344202} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dafa4e-91ab-5e93-a865-b1e60c56b9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2({e}^{6}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344272}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344151} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3865ea-0943-5557-a486-c9bc91ea15eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e2968de0-3f24-5e15-95ba-b17804d225c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-76650" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s\n$$ along the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{C}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{t}_{a}}^{{t}_{b}}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t,\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3).$ \n\nThe position vectors of the endpoints of our line segment are $a=j$ and $b=2\\,i+3\\,j.$ So, the parametrization of the line segment is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+t\\,(b-a) \\\\  & =j+t[2\\,i+3\\,j-j] \\\\  & =j+t(2\\,i+2\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\,i+(1+2t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $0\\le t\\le 1.$ Thus, we have that $x=2t$ and $y=1+2t$ along the segment.\n\nComputing ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+2t)j \\\\  & =2\\,i+2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\right)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x,y)=(4x+2)\\,{e}^{xy}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(r(t)) & =(4(2t)+2){e}^{2t(1+2t)} \\\\  & =(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f(r(t))\\,‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this using the substitution $u=2t+4{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=(2+8t)\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(8t+2)\\,{e}^{2t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =2\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{6}{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{e}^{u}\\,{|}_{0}^{6} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-{e}^{0}) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}({e}^{6}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345273 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01be4f6a-7433-5314-a608-48fcf05abf08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342265} {:answer/id #uuid "1e9cda28-6297-54d7-b4e2-4171e73d5906" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342493} {:answer/id #uuid "82ef60dd-2cb8-55f6-bf7e-b80ad50dffc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343360} {:answer/id #uuid "f22869e4-b606-516e-b14a-50b77a6552ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344367} {:answer/id #uuid "f59b9d82-ed5b-54e1-a7fd-5d05287eabfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344398}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186344398} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f22b33b-13c8-5335-b756-7298551d2c82" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ee8a9ed-bba6-55f8-8e47-b79aa79ab0b7" :question/math-academy-id "q-77125" :question/problem "![](images/a4/a42c95d0604d9855fb00ac9b7f2a1d2f9db0dd9c6beb3b68750a074818c99dc8.png)\n\nWhich of the following is equivalent to the integral $$\n\\displaystyle {\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r,\n$$ where $C$ is the closed polygonal path $ODEO,$ shown in the diagram above, traversed in the counterclockwise direction?" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that we can represent $C,$ which is traversed in counterclockwise, as the union of $3$ paths: \n\n$$\nC={C}_{1}\\cup {-{C}_{2}}\\cup {-{C}_{3}},\n$$\n\nwhere $-{C}_{2}$ and $-{C}_{3}$ are the same paths as ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ but traversed in the opposite direction.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{C}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{-{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345274 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "013ef795-942d-50f4-83dc-15aab4fbe0c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342257} {:answer/id #uuid "3c789914-0f4b-5e38-a6be-25a97983968e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342736} {:answer/id #uuid "9c29e367-5a7d-5e08-a8c4-ffe4554e420a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343615} {:answer/id #uuid "b0325901-d312-55e8-994f-b53760b697b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343787} {:answer/id #uuid "c081d7e3-54be-5608-bc89-650e95c065c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{\\int }_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343927}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342257} :answer-field/id #uuid "19c651ff-ec09-54bc-bcdf-8c29a3729b89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344537}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "1a475ad3-f062-505d-a7c3-573f5f8f58ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-77131" :question/problem "![](images/54/5484647a9c69